<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Koala on Kunal Kushwaha</title><link>https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/tags/koala/</link><description>Recent content in Koala on Kunal Kushwaha</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0900</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/tags/koala/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Before I Trust an Agent, I Try to Break Its Box</title><link>https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/2026/11/06/before-i-trust-an-agent/</link><pubDate>Fri, 06 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0900</pubDate><guid>https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/2026/11/06/before-i-trust-an-agent/</guid><description>&lt;figure&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="fig-card"&gt;&lt;img src="hero.jpg" alt="Untrusted code runs inside the VM. The host stays outside the guest boundary." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;Untrusted code runs inside the VM. The host stays outside the guest boundary.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;In October I wrote that Koala should &lt;a href="https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/2026/10/17/give-every-agent-its-own-room/"&gt;give every agent its own room&lt;/a&gt;: one Linux VM per workload, with the network check and the credentials kept on the host. That is a design claim. Claims are cheap.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>A Clean Room Is Not a Home</title><link>https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/2026/10/30/a-clean-room-is-not-a-home/</link><pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0900</pubDate><guid>https://kunalkushwaha.github.io/2026/10/30/a-clean-room-is-not-a-home/</guid><description>&lt;figure&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="fig-card"&gt;&lt;img src="hero.png" alt="A Clean Room Is Not a Home. On the left, an empty glass box with a desk and a checklist. On the right, an agent works in its own lit room in a building, with keys locked in a vault below and every connection to the outside passing through a guarded gate." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;A Clean Room Is Not a Home. On the left, an empty glass box with a desk and a checklist. On the right, an agent works in its own lit room in a building, with keys locked in a vault below and every connection to the outside passing through a guarded gate.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;When I hear &amp;ldquo;sandbox&amp;rdquo; today, the picture is usually a coding agent: a container, a repo, run the tests, throw it away. That works. But an agent that answers email or runs a workflow for months is not a coding agent with a longer timeout. It lives somewhere.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>